  "design": {
    "decision": "Lead low [♦]s while opponents still hold [♦] (each one now wins), follow a [♦] lead with your lowest [♦] instead of the honor, and bid [♦] length as tricks.",
    "opponent": "Revealed the first time a low [♦] takes an opponent's honor. From then on opponents can only win [♦] tricks by voiding and trumping, so they discard [♦]s early.",
    "rationale": "This is a card-strength rule on a narrow card set, the family the brief says measures well. I would buy it before knowing my build, because any [♦]s become sure winners in [♦] tricks. The brief's lesson about low ranks applies to [♠], where low cards become top trumps. A side suit never beats trumps, so the counterplay of voiding and trumping stays intact. Combined with the kept untrumpable [♦] it makes the [♦] length line a real plan. My estimate puts it close to uc-twos-beat (+14.6). A rule has no amount to retune, so it may need to move to rare.",
    "partners": [
      "ua-diamonds-untrumpable",
      "r2-diamond-lead-mult",
      "seed-diamond-win",
      "e-win-suit-d-mult",
      "ca-diamond-led-win"
    ],
    "cluster": "R2",
    "draftText": "Your team's [♦]s beat every other card of their suit",
